WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.880
All the warning signs were there - but they
were ignored for decades.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:16.420
The alarm bells really went off this year–
hopefully not too late!

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:21.640
The Colorado River – the direct lifeline
for more than 42 million people, and indirectly

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:24.370
for tens of millions more is in peril.

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:28.320
“It's really bad right now and it's only
supposed to get worse next year.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.210
You know, the fundamental aspect to this,
though, is that we experts, and decision makers,

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:36.390
we've known for decades that there's this
challenge on the Colorado River.

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:41.070
We've known that we take more water out of
the system and the river can actually supply,

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:44.969
but we've known that.”

00:00:44.969 --> 00:00:49.430
” In the west of the United States water
is the new gold, the most sought-after commodity.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:56.390
For the states of Utah and Wyoming, for Arizona,
California, for Nevada, New Mexico, and Colorado

00:00:56.390 --> 00:01:00.660
water has become a huge problem.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:05.640
The same goes for dozens of native American
tribes and communities of indigenous people.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:10.100
All face harsh water cutbacks and tremendous
challenges in the future.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:14.659
Things are so bad- the federal government
had to step in.

00:01:14.659 --> 00:01:19.780
Washington DC - The Department of Interior
and its’ Federal Bureau of Reclamation.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:25.070
In an audio only-press conference – a sound
assessment of what is now slugged a drought

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:30.960
or water crisis and of what has become a top
priority for the White House.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:36.469
“The prolonged drought afflicting the West
is one of the most significant challenges

00:01:36.469 --> 00:01:39.300
facing our communities in our country.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:45.579
Right now, almost 93% of the western United
States is experiencing drought, or abnormally

00:01:45.579 --> 00:01:52.670
dry conditions, with more than 70% of the
western United States experiencing severe

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:57.440
or extreme drought conditions.”

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:02.189
Those “prolonged” drought conditions manifest
even in the Rocky Mountains.

00:02:02.189 --> 00:02:06.450
Global warming and climate change make for
very mild winters that don’t produce enough

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:11.190
snow melt anymore for the Colorado River to
carry downstream into Lake Powell, Lake Mead

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:12.370
and beyond.

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:17.070
“The Colorado River Basin is in its 23rd
year of a historic drought.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:23.620
Both Lake Powell and Lake Mead the two largest
reservoirs in the United States are at historically

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:27.489
low levels with combined storage of 28% of
their capacity.

00:02:27.489 --> 00:02:31.730
The system is approaching a tipping point.

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:36.959
And without action, we cannot protect the
system for the millions of Americans who rely

00:02:36.959 --> 00:02:38.620
on this critical resource.”

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:43.610
We’re meeting with Christian Gerlach, a
community activist and senior representative

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:47.099
of national environmental organization the
“Sierra Club”.

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:52.510
Christian wants to show us a place called
Echo Bay within what is known as Lake Mead

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:56.520
National Recreational Area – just a few
miles east of Las Vegas.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.730
“Hardly anybody ever comes to Echo Bay unfortunately.

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:05.049
It was very popular spot, so popular in fact
it had an airport, but as you can see the

00:03:05.049 --> 00:03:09.700
water levels dropped so dramatically, it’s
effected the economy of the area.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:14.240
There are not people coming out to visit and
to recreate as much here in Echo Bay.”

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:19.659
Echo Bay is not the only spot falling victim
to unprecedented and record low water levels

00:03:19.659 --> 00:03:21.829
of Lake Mead.

00:03:21.829 --> 00:03:27.660
From where the water stood in 2008, Mead visitors
now must drive almost 1093 yards, or a kilometer

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:29.950
more, until they can launch their boats.

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:34.430
Besides, there’s the only one boat ramp
left open.

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:36.540
Years ago, there were 12.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:42.629
The Marina, once a bustling hot spot for boaters
and water sport enthusiasts has more mostly

00:03:42.629 --> 00:03:45.670
empty parking space now than ever before.

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:51.730
At Lake Mead - created by the Hoover Dam,
- one can see and experience what it means

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:55.560
when experts say “the system is at a tipping
point”.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.969
“I’d be concerned.

00:03:58.969 --> 00:04:03.379
Seems lower than average and global warming
has something going on with it.”

00:04:03.379 --> 00:04:08.219
Christina Taub - a flight attendant from Germany
- also visits Hoover Dam, she is alarmed by

00:04:08.219 --> 00:04:09.250
what she observed.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:13.370
“I think the bell should have rung a long
time ago and it should keep ringing.

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:15.900
Something has to be done about it.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:17.989
It is very dangerously low.

00:04:17.989 --> 00:04:22.001
And we’re actually
part of a flight crew and we flew over a few

00:04:22.001 --> 00:04:26.870
days ago and just to see it also from above
at 40,000 feet – I can show you pictures,

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:29.040
it’s unbelievable!”

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:35.190
Mead’s water level shrank by a whopping
150-plus feet in the last 40 years.

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:41.440
An ever-growing so-called white bathtub ring
here and at other reservoirs will have devastating

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:47.590
effects for all US states in the lower Colorado
River Basin, for tens of millions of people

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:52.880
and hundreds of thousands of businesses in
Nevada, Arizona, California and even in Mexico.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:58.490
“The impacts we're seeing from climate change
are I think, you know, most of what we are

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:02.660
more are happening quicker than we would have
expected.

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:11.350
And things the impacts of snowpack translating
into flows in the river are being impacted

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:17.250
by climate change things like soil moisture,
temperatures, evapo-transpiration, all these

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:21.580
little mechanisms, all these little components
to the hydrologic cycle.

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:24.740
Here in the West, are being squeezed by climate
change.”

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:29.960
“We are on track to see additional drops
in the water levels, to the point where it’s

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:30.960
scary.

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:35.130
We may face what is called “dead pool”,
where water will not even flow out of Lake

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:41.380
Mead further down the Colorado River system.”

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:44.900
No shortage of water in so-called Sin City.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:50.250
The expansive water fountain display in front
of Las Vegas’ iconic Hotel Bellagio is just

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:51.360
one example.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.030
What a waste?

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:54.200
Not so fast!

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:59.930
Las Vegas, boom town and self-declared entertainment
capital of the world is in fact top of the

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:04.160
line when it comes to water recycling, preservation
and conservation.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:11.650
“So, places like Las Vegas, have actually
done amazing efforts in terms of water conservation.

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:17.530
If every city that relied on Colorado River
water would do as Las Vegas has done, our

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:19.430
cities would be in a much better spot.

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:22.950
Las Vegas has really been a leader when it
comes to water conservation.

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:28.300
So, things like the pools you're looking at,
like the big fountains in front of Bellagio

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:33.060
that's recycled water that's not potable water.”

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:38.550
In Las Vegas pictures like this- sprinklers
irrigating golf courses or public parks - won’t

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:40.120
make for a lot of fuss.

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:44.760
All water used is recycled, re-used over and
over again.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:49.490
In the cities suburbs homeowners rip out the
grass in their front and back yards and replace

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:51.760
it with gravel or fake grass.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:56.470
Throughout the city so called “no-functional
turf grass” is banned now.

00:06:56.470 --> 00:07:00.820
Most areas of turf that require a lot of irrigation
have been replaced.

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:05.500
“The Southern Nevada Water Authority has
taken tremendous steps toward reducing the

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:07.190
water usage.

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:11.210
In the past 20 years, the state of Nevada
has seen tremendous growth in the Las Vegas

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:12.210
Valley.

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:15.080
But we've actually seen a decrease in our
water usage.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:20.240
Thanks to programs that help people take out
their water thirsty lawns.”

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:24.940
That’s good news of course and those measures
are critically important.

00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:31.460
However, they won’t make a difference big
enough to create change on a larger scale

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:35.310
needed to prevent even worse drought and water
conditions in the future.

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:40.810
“We do need to make hard cuts that that's
the fundamental solution on the Colorado River

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:48.610
is we have to use less water and you know
the scale of how much we need to use less

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:50.730
is millions of acre feet.

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:57.770
So, you know, the Colorado River on average,
in the last 20 years, it's applied like 13-to-14-million-acre

00:07:57.770 --> 00:08:00.670
feet per year 13 to 14 million acre feet.

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:05.820
You know, the Bureau of Reclamation who manages
the system is telling us we need to cut 2-to-4-million-acre

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:06.820
feet.

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:14.990
So that's like 15 to 30% of how much water
we use on economy needs to be cut immediately.

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:17.270
So that's a lot of water.

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:20.520
That's a big scale.”

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:26.340
Experts warn that Only unprecedented big scale
water use reductions, strict water conservation,

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:31.990
and ultimately the de-carbonization of our
economies – will secure the Colorado River

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:37.090
as a life source for tens of millions of people
and for hundreds of thousands of businesses.

00:08:37.090 --> 00:08:43.099
“We got to decarbonize our economies, we've
got to address the situation of the carbon

00:08:43.099 --> 00:08:46.480
cycle which is intricately linked to the natural
water cycle.

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:52.310
There is tremendous amounts more carbon in
the atmosphere than there was several decades

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:53.310
ago.

00:08:53.310 --> 00:08:57.889
And that trend continues on and as we see
that we will see additional heat waves we

00:08:57.889 --> 00:09:03.709
will see this additional heat urban island
effect in suburban areas where we do have

00:09:03.709 --> 00:09:08.180
more asphalt when it rains, especially here
in Las Vegas.

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:13.439
A lot of asphalt funnels the water towards
Lake Mead, but a lot of it is lost due to

00:09:13.439 --> 00:09:16.529
evaporation it no longer penetrates the water
table.

00:09:16.529 --> 00:09:19.699
So, it's all an interconnected system.

00:09:19.699 --> 00:09:25.410
And we've got to take a look at our developed
society is currently operating to make those

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:28.490
changes.”

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:34.100
Making those - perhaps painful but strongly
needed changes is not a can-do any longer,

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:36.390
but a must-do, experts say.

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:41.889
Otherwise the mighty Colorado River is feared
to become not much more than a trickle – rather

00:09:41.889 --> 00:09:43.190
sooner than later.

